WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.240
- One of your first acting jobs

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:03.540
is actually dubbing cartoons?

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.840
- Not dubbing cartoons, but

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.360
dubbing foreign film.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.560
I mean, when I was growing up on TV,

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.780
they would have these
kind of gladiator films

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.693
or Speed Racer cartoons.

00:00:15.693 --> 00:00:16.526
- Yeah.

00:00:16.526 --> 00:00:18.019
- Right, Speed Racer.

00:00:18.019 --> 00:00:20.350
And so I got a job dubbing voices

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:21.730
in foreign films.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.630
And if you don't know what dubbing is,

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.280
and we do it in American
TV and movies too,

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.670
if the sound is bad, you
gotta go into a studio

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.290
and then re-do the sentence.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:33.935
Like for instance, let's do it now.

00:00:33.935 --> 00:00:34.768
- Okay. Let's do it.

00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:35.601
- Okay, so--

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:36.434
- Ooh, I like it.

00:00:36.434 --> 00:00:37.268
It's a game. I'm in.

00:00:37.268 --> 00:00:39.880
Yeah. (laughter) That's my energy.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.420
- So exciting. Contagious.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:42.393
- Yeah, sorry.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:46.820
- So now just say this line.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:51.010
Say the line, "Melvin, I
have to devein the shrimp."

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:51.843
Just say it.

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:54.710
- Melvin, I have to devein the shrimp.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:56.835
- Now I heard a sound, so
we have to take that out.

00:00:56.835 --> 00:00:58.593
You have to...

00:00:58.593 --> 00:00:59.800
- Open my mouth.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.240
- But it's in another language

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.080
so I have to find the line to

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.533
fit the same kind of mouth flaps,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:07.980
but I have to put in a line

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.930
that's germane to whatever
film I'm trying to...

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:12.470
- So like if I'm going to mouth that...

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:14.023
- Say Melvin...what did you have to say?

00:01:14.023 --> 00:01:16.150
- Melvin, I have to devein the shrimp.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.820
- So now don't do any voice now.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.930
Beep, beep, beep.

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:22.358
There's a monster in the living room.

00:01:22.358 --> 00:01:24.608
(laughter)

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:28.603
- Listen...

00:01:30.569 --> 00:01:32.743
- I wasn't ready for that.
- You're a terrible beeper.

00:01:33.945 --> 00:01:37.015
- I wasn't ready for the
(inaudible), I wasn't

00:01:37.015 --> 00:01:37.848
ready for that.

00:01:37.848 --> 00:01:39.600
- You have to approximate your
character and your whole...

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:40.433
- No.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:41.410
- That's amazingly hard.

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:42.300
- Let's do it again.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.490
- I can't look at you.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:44.440
- Don't look at me.

00:01:47.032 --> 00:01:47.865
- Melvin, I have to devein this shrimp.

00:01:47.865 --> 00:01:48.698
- If I had a nickel for
every time I've heard that.

00:01:48.698 --> 00:01:49.531
All right now listen, I'm
going to count down, ready?

00:01:49.531 --> 00:01:50.364
- Okay.

00:01:50.364 --> 00:01:51.440
- Beep, beep, beep.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.225
There's a monster in the living room.

00:01:54.225 --> 00:01:55.130
(laughter and clapping)

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:55.963
See?

00:01:55.963 --> 00:01:57.133
So that's it.

00:01:57.133 --> 00:01:58.240
(laughter and clapping)

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.510
- So randomly enough, I walked
in to watch my daughter.

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:04.400
She's five, and I let her just pick

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.920
something on Netflix.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:07.300
I was doing something.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:08.650
All of a sudden, I'm like, why

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:10.280
are they speaking Spanish?

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.980
She watches things that are from Spanish,

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.290
but they'll try to do them in English.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:15.250
Half the episode is English.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:16.630
They do that a lot, still.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:19.030
- Yes. Part of the problem is that

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:21.300
in different languages
there's different cadences,

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.133
and different rhythms.

00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:23.134
- Yeah, rhythm.

00:02:23.134 --> 00:02:24.040
- So that's why if you
watch a lot of old movies,

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.810
you'll hear people talking
like this over there

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:29.550
because they try to fit in dialog

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.010
into foreign mouth flapping.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.140
- Oh, my God. That's hard.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.490
And hard not to laugh, obviously.

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:37.530
- Hard not to laugh.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:38.363
You need time.

00:02:38.363 --> 00:02:39.400
- I know, I know.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.360
You know what? I should just steer clear.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:42.193
- Forget it.

00:02:42.193 --> 00:02:43.030
- I feel like I should forget it.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:44.243
- You're doing too much already.

